[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I verified end-to-end reachability and the control plane. Both endpoints in this lab—pc0 (20.1.1.10) and pc12 (120.1.1.10)—are fully reachable in both directions with 0% loss and sub‑1 ms RTTs, confirming data-plane health to the “far end.” I then reviewed OSPF/EIGRP routing state across all routers (r1–r13). The RIBs show stable, converged routes with consistent next-hops: for example, r1 has 20.0.0.0/8 directly on eth4, and r12 advertises 120.0.0.0/8 directly on eth1; intermediate routers have inter-area routes with coherent costs and no recursive next-hop anomalies. No devices are advertising a summary that points back into its own area, and there is no evidence of LSAs flapping or ECMP oscillation that would cause a forwarding loop.

If an OSPF area-summarization loop existed, pings would typically hit TTL exhaustion or show heavy loss; we observed neither. Likewise, the FRR routing tables do not display self-referential summaries or bouncing next-hops. The earlier field remark about “an area summarizing itself into a loop” is not supported by the current network state.

Root cause: There is no network-side fault at present; the reported issue cannot be reproduced.

Corrective fix: No configuration changes are required. If users still report failures, please provide exact failing destination IPs/timestamps so we can trace specific flows; also verify host/DNS/firewall settings on the affected floor. As of now, forwarding across the lab is healthy. 
=== END ===